course: dictionary
lesson: 3429
title: Extraordinary and ordinary
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Extraordinary means very unusual, surprising or special. Ordinary means normal, so extraordinary means much more than ordinary. You can spot the opposite in the word itself: extra means more, so the word goes beyond the normal.
explain: «Extraordinary claims need strong evidence.» The clue is strong evidence. People need good proof before they believe something surprising. Ask: what would fit here? Try swapping in very unusual and see if the sentence still makes sense. It does.
explain: Extraordinary is also used to praise. «Extraordinary talent» means talent that is very great, far beyond what most people have. When you meet this word, look for the clue that tells you whether something is surprising, very special, or very good.
fact: Extraordinary means very unusual, surprising or special.
fact: «Extraordinary» means much more than ordinary.
fact: Phrases you may meet include «an extraordinary story», «extraordinary courage» and «quite extraordinary».
fact: In a more formal sense, extraordinary means extra or special, outside the normal plan, as in «an extraordinary meeting».
fact: «Extraordinary claims need strong evidence.» This means a surprising claim needs good proof before people accept it.
exercise: What does extraordinary mean? | Very small and quiet | Very ordinary and normal | *Very unusual and surprising
exercise: «Extraordinary claims need strong evidence.» What do extraordinary claims need? | Loud voices | A long nap | *Strong evidence
exercise: Find and copy one word that means very great or very good: «extraordinary talent» | talent | *extraordinary | very | good
test: What does «extraordinary courage» show? | Ordinary tiredness | Very small fear | *Very great bravery
test: Ada says extraordinary is just another word for ordinary. Why is she wrong? | Ordinary and extraordinary mean the same | Extraordinary means very small | *Ordinary means normal, but extraordinary means much more than normal
exam: Which sentence shows something very unusual or special? | Rain fell on the roof all day | The bus was on time as usual | We ate toast for breakfast | *The dancer made an extraordinary leap that amazed the whole crowd
